<feed xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><title>Breaking news from AFRICA, Maghreb and Sub-Saharan Africa - France 24</title><subtitle type="html">FRANCE 24’s coverage of events, politics and social issues across Africa, including North Africa (the Maghreb) and the Sahel, aided by our network of correspondents and experts across the continent.</subtitle><id>https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:35:43Z</updated><author><name>rssync</name></author><link rel="self" href="rss" type="application/atom+xml" /><link rel="alternate" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/rss" type="application/rss+xml" /><entry><title>37 die in Nigerian custody: Reports blame overcrowding, government disputes claim</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1c44790c0f8d650d32b434a6d19572632b161eb321554609a651790ce59de853</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:35:26Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T11:26:22Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/37-die-in-nigerian-custody-reports-blame-overcrowding-government-disputes-claim--2875a91ef8ec.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/37-die-in-nigerian-custody-reports-blame-overcrowding-government-disputes-claim" /><summary type="html">Authorities in north-central Nigeria are investigating the deaths of 37 people while they were in custody for illegal mining. Officials say the detainees died at a detention facility in Niger state, with the Nigerian Security and Civil Defence Corps pointing to a possible disease outbreak. However, some reports suggest overcrowding and poor ventilation may have contributed to the deaths. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent Sam Olukoya.</summary></entry><entry><title>Suspected alcohol poisoning in Nigeria's Ondo State kills at least 49 people</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:4a4ee6820c5ab2d4b1dbf0c5fade716670e84e27aeeb495ed5e953299c10ff0c</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:35:29Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T11:19:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/suspected-alcohol-poisoning-in-nigeria-s-ondo-state-kills-at-least-49-people--5aaebcf6dccb.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/suspected-alcohol-poisoning-in-nigeria-s-ondo-state-kills-at-least-49-people" /><summary type="html">In Nigeria's Ondo state, at least 49 people have died and 100 others injured after drinking alcohol suspected to contain methanol. Local authorities say 'Monkey Shoulder', a locally brewed herbal drink with high alcohol content is believed to have been the source of the poisoning, but investigations are still ongoing. State police have announced the arrest of 15 people in connection with the incident. Story by Clemence Waller.</summary></entry><entry><title>Ebola outbreak reaches new areas in Democratic Republic of Congo</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:b8db0b2bd89d8b18cf70a07dee88ba51358789aa25562ca6170af4d4afc0dadb</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:35:43Z</updated><published>2026-09-19T09:59:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ebola-outbreak-reaches-new-areas-in-democratic-republic-of-congo--c0ca8e99988f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ebola-outbreak-reaches-new-areas-in-democratic-republic-of-congo" /><summary type="html">In the Democratic Republic of Congo, the government claims that the Ebola outbreak has changed course, with cases trending downwards in hotspots. But that interpretation is being met with pushback from experts and health responders, who say the outbreak remains as bad as ever. Details by FRANCE 24 correspondent Emmet Livingstone.</summary></entry><entry><title>Uproar in Niger state, 37 die in custody</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:af2ab3be0e1c87821059cd32c55fc5e299c65ad661721dc3e71762a3fa6be8e2</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-18T20:52:51Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/uproar-in-niger-state-37-die-in-custody--9b3070eb9dd2.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/uproar-in-niger-state-37-die-in-custody" /><summary type="html">In tonights programme, investigation is ongoing into the death in custody of thirty-seven people, accused of illegal mining in Nigeria. Also,Tunisia is emerging as a major boat-building hub, serving both local buyers and international markets. And finally, we speak to the author of “The Moon Will Not Burn Us”, Ricci Shryock.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>At least 37 suspected illegal miners die in custody in central Nigeria</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ed5c2cbfba934f414e23440a3a72fc2099a47019abaa081132b04a5629adb523</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-18T00:27:53Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260918-37-suspected-illegal-miners-die-in-custody-in-central-nigeria--1d616cfc5edd.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260918-37-suspected-illegal-miners-die-in-custody-in-central-nigeria" /><summary type="html">Nigerian authorities launched an investigation Thursday after 37 suspected illegal miners died in a civil defence detention facility in central Niger state, with conflicting accounts blaming either a disease outbreak or overcrowding and poor ventilation.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Serial killer suspicions in South Africa</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:1578ce212ed93beba2c4b46f8eba683c5db5a50024b22b5cf21212a04032c408</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T20:25:05Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/serial-killer-suspicions-in-south-africa--a7d4c3810954.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/serial-killer-suspicions-in-south-africa" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: the body of a ninth woman is found in Johannesburg as South African police start investigating a potential femicidal serial killer. Also, Africans are excited about what good use of the technology could mean to growth, but many are also worried about what an AI free-for-all could mean for their lives and livelihoods. And Chad’s first music school officially opened its doors on Wednesday night in N’Djamena.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>DR Congo says Ebola outbreak has passed its peak</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:13ae96cfa9691f103c311f2caaf64dfaecab63f83d29a517fe41619596051850</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T13:36:09Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/dr-congo-says-ebola-outbreak-has-passed-its-peak--314bad08c0b7.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/dr-congo-says-ebola-outbreak-has-passed-its-peak" /><summary type="html">More than 3,510 people have died from Ebola in eastern DR Congo since the outbreak was declared four months ago, with Ituri accounting for 78% of confirmed cases. Congolese authorities say the epidemic has now passed its peak, pointing to signs that the outbreak is beginning to slow.</summary></entry><entry><title>Zimbabwe farmers brace for El Niño by saving water</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:9c445f814cde66f322fb40e1a9fa59314b907879feb6a8686fc7fb77432aceb0</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T13:33:33Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/video/20260917-zimbabwe-farmers-brace-for-el-ni_o-by-saving-water--f6a1044bac7d.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/video/20260917-zimbabwe-farmers-brace-for-el-ni%C3%B1o-by-saving-water" /><summary type="html">Some communities in Zimbabwe are adopting water-saving techniques such as aquaponics and rainwater harvesting to protect crops as the country braces for El Niño-driven drought. Sharon Mazingaizo has this report.</summary></entry><entry><title>Tigray: 20,000 university students relocated by conflict</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:daf7619002df0c9216086183c458839e30046e0ac692f0408fae7a9de1f1f05e</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T13:30:51Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tigray-20-000-university-students-relocated-by-conflict--789fed3e7a55.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tigray-20-000-university-students-relocated-by-conflict" /><summary type="html">Ethiopia's Education Ministry says around 20,000 university students in Tigray will be temporarily relocated to other public campuses after accusing the TPLF of using universities as military bases. The move comes as fighting and drone strikes continue in the northern region, with many students saying the trauma of the 2020-2022 Tigray war is still fresh. Our correspondent Tom Canetti reports from Addis Ababa.</summary></entry><entry><title>Senegal farmers turn to fish and rice farming</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:372b550533a1125b9f6a13f79c56a4296651159cecb08bc93b749ac1c7ed15ac</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-17T13:28:56Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/senegal-farmers-turn-to-fish-and-rice-farming--f779267cbc29.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/senegal-farmers-turn-to-fish-and-rice-farming" /><summary type="html">Some farmers in northern Senegal are combining fish farming with rice cultivation, using an age-old technique that helps fertilise fields, reduce pests and diversify incomes. The climate-smart approach is gaining renewed attention as communities adapt to growing environmental pressures.</summary></entry><entry><title>Encouraging signs in DR Congo Ebola fight</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:14f8971e4d3399b2c428528659fe3c074ed918e41d48ccdfd4b0b7e605074294</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-16T20:42:27Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-fight--de2796e18743.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-fight" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: there are signs of a welcome slowdown in DR Congo's Ebola outbreak. Also, about 20,000 Ethiopian university students in the northern region of Tigray will be relocated to other campuses across the country. And in northern Senegal, scientists are using fish to keep their food stocks and communtites healthy.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>WHO sees 'encouraging signs' in DR Congo Ebola response but says outbreak 'far from over'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:582c8c6571f97c7dde9ffcef1ce74efc3229dd76ee8aa81f8472d94e5f20cbda</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-16T15:29:37Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-who-sees-encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-response-but-says-outbreak-far-from-over--b7a7c127a44a.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-who-sees-encouraging-signs-in-dr-congo-ebola-response-but-says-outbreak-far-from-over" /><summary type="html">The World Health Organization said Wednesday that the fight against the Ebola outbreak in DR Congo was showing “encouraging signs" but that the epidemic was "far from over". More than 7,000 cases have been reported across seven provinces as of September 11.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>At least 82 killed in gold mine collapse in remote area of Sudan, paramilitary RSF say</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:21c9a55478001d327f72af7de88022973b312118025d1a57a85ae80ad4491d7b</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-16T11:54:54Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-sudan-gold-mine-collapse-kills-at-least-67-people--0a2ff1999c68.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260916-sudan-gold-mine-collapse-kills-at-least-67-people" /><summary type="html">A gold mine collapse on Wednesday killed at least 82 people in a remote area in Sudan, a source in the paramilitary Rapid Support Forces reported. The destruction occurred at the al-Zara gold mine, located in West Kordofan province – an area under the control of the RSF, who have been at war with the country’s military for over three years.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Russian war, African recruits</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:7871f941af594ac877486db69c924fb1a01eed66879234fa5ce0824e0ba27c09</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-15T22:13:20Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/russian-war-african-recruits--06e9911a8624.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/russian-war-african-recruits" /><summary type="html">In this edition: thousands of Africans have found themselves in Ukraine fighting or working on behalf of the Kremlin. We speak to a journalist who just got back from Ukraine. Also, in the Democratic Republic of Congo, the C64 opposition coalition held a demonstration in the capital Kinshasa. And finally, Kenya's celebrating a stellar athletics victory as the country beats London in the race to host the 2029 World Championships.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria's Dangote refinery launches biggest African IPO</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cdfd574e4132945b2d468e4704403abc7621617dca3861bf9026a7b57357ce42</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-15T00:05:32Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260915-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-biggest-african-ipo-for-retail-investors--d05fe90057ad.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260915-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-biggest-african-ipo-for-retail-investors" /><summary type="html">Africa’s richest man Aliko Dangote on Monday launched an IPO for his $19 billion Nigerian oil refinery, seeking $1.6 billion from retail investors across the continent. The offering values Africa’s largest refinery at about $49 billion and has drawn both strong investor interest and concerns over its high valuation.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nigerian oil refinery Dangote aims to raise 1.6 billion dollars</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ba85a0e4c281104bdd16faa00cd16907c577c898049d907c29d19f5ffb42e8a3</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-14T20:31:28Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/nigerian-oil-refinery-dangote-aims-to-raise-1-6-billion-dollars--8328c51224d1.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/nigerian-oil-refinery-dangote-aims-to-raise-1-6-billion-dollars" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: Nigeria's Dangote refinery opens itself to public ownership with plans to raise $1.6 billion dollars from retail investors across the continent. Also, DR Congo’s President Felix Tshisekedi calls on the National Service to clean up the streets of Kinshasa. And with a month and a half to go until the Youth Olympic Games begin in Senegal, the Olympic flame begins its tour of the host country.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Nigeria's Dangote refinery launches continent's biggest IPO</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:33c19efa2b113099f7d46370e2bacec9e9fa0b3773aac0d2304aa1f28760918d</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-14T10:55:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260914-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-continent-s-biggest-ipo--ce4a59a9b03f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260914-nigeria-s-dangote-refinery-launches-continent-s-biggest-ipo" /><summary type="html">Nigerian billionaire Aliko Dangote on Monday launched what is slated to be Africa's biggest initial public offering (IPO), aiming to raise $1.6 billion in part to expand his mega oil refinery into the world's largest.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Children die in a school fire in Bukavu</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:725a1beb1f0846ed6066a1b8f582e7dbf77c648cd8e144f033b158eccdba3c56</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-11T20:18:17Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260911-children-die-in-a-school-fire-in-bukavu--f4db6b01d2b9.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/eye-on-africa/20260911-children-die-in-a-school-fire-in-bukavu" /><summary type="html">In this edition: children have died after a school fire broke out in the M23 rebel occupied city of Bukavu, Democratic Republic of Congo. Also, Senegal looks to the skies as it seeks to develop its home-grown satellite industry. And finally, we wonder about the future of African cities. How will they cope with the population boom and climate crisis? Some at the Kenya architecture biennale in Nairobi tried to answer these questions.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Ugandan royal succession scandal</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:6e374fb5b812ad82fe50d959ada85832814d52fe832a1ddcdcc99bde31998810</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T20:20:01Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal--dc969309a6f8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/ugandan-royal-succession-scandal" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, a dramatic struggle for control of a throne in Uganda. Also, Rwanda shut down 101 alcoholic beverage factories last month and declared a public health crisis after at least 50 people were killed by contaminated illegal supplies. And Ethiopia celebrates its new year.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Infantino is the one who has brought the most to Africa' says Samuel Eto'o</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:e688fa32aab37305b95b8afbb2328620af7d90f156d2960070644304e18aa20b</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T14:31:49Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t_te-_-t_te/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o--38a56194e182.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/t%C3%AAte-%C3%A0-t%C3%AAte/20260910-infantino-is-the-one-who-has-brought-the-most-to-africa-says-samuel-eto-o" /><summary type="html">In an interview with FRANCE 24, former football star and current president of the Cameroonian Football Federation Samuel Eto'o spoke about the recent backlash towards FIFA president Gianni Infantino and confirmed he will support him for re-election because of "everything he has brought to African football", adding that the African federations will defend what seems "right to us given the challenges that we face in our own federations".</summary><category term="Tête à tête" /></entry><entry><title>Algeria cuts diplomatic ties with United Arab Emirates over 'provocative and hostile actions'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37eeec9672f2263dbd1520ca79f854fbd758bed0e760d4c643fc57e820db2ad5</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-10T11:50:14Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions--dd9c4cc89c40.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260910-algeria-cuts-diplomatic-ties-with-united-arab-emirates-over-provocative-and-hostile-actions" /><summary type="html">Algeria is severing diplomatic ties with the United Arab Emirates, state media reported Thursday. Algerian President ‌Abdelmadjid Tebboune has in the past accused the Gulf state of trying to meddle in Algeria's internal affairs.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Aid cuts force clinic closures across Sudan</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:d6e5e77d1a4ed73c56c3287c89e79fd1c3655baf2191ec0981793e6a4c0a90fc</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T20:32:55Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan--1bc1aa71732f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/aid-cuts-force-clinic-closures-across-sudan" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: aid groups are warning that funding cuts are forcing dozens of health clinics in Sudan to close. Also, an AI and Africa conference has kicked off in South Korea's capital. Finally, we take a look at the Rwanda Space Agency as Paris hosts its very first international space summit.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>JNIM jihadists step up incursions into northern Benin</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:8cb7c2803851408c08a106650d367d9be0d4881198f5080c3113f75f24e4922b</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-09T10:00:18Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260909-jnim-jihadists-step-up-incursions-northern-benin--c61528c3ec0c.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/the-observers/20260909-jnim-jihadists-step-up-incursions-northern-benin" /><summary type="html">Al Qaeda affiliate JNIM has been intensifying its activities in Benin since 2022. The country serves as a transit corridor between the areas of operation of the group, which is well established in Mali, Niger and Burkina Faso. Two nature reserves have become logistical hubs for the jihadist fighters. Benin's army is striving to hold the line.</summary><category term="The Observers" /></entry><entry><title>Kenya cracks down on foreign traders</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:cddc82fd6982e20aeb251a05a222b2134e46372ea5a7500855e77c9133d96b0b</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-08T20:29:13Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/kenya-cracks-down-on-foreign-traders--c31b8c5d613f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/kenya-cracks-down-on-foreign-traders" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition, Hundreds of Burundians flocked to ⁠their embassy in ​Nairobi to get travel documents to return home. Also, in his first speech to the National assembly, Senegal’s Prime Minister Ahmadou Al Aminou Lo slams the rise of xenophobic rhetoric on the continent. Also, FRANCE 24's Georja Calvin Smith speaks to the founder of an AI surgical non-profit about the work being done in Tanzania to extend the reach of the country's 27 overburdened neurosurgeons.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian influencer faces death penalty over drug charges</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f1cf797285b00ea8e50cc64249629787c2a1d3e29d652a71b72839d47ab9d275</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T20:27:19Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges--5338612a10b4.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/egyptian-influencer-s-death-penalty-over-drug-charges" /><summary type="html">In tonight's edition: An Egyptian TV presenter is among a dozen people a court has sentenced to death on drug charges. But first: a South African high court rejects a former defence minister's bid to have a corruption case against her dropped. Plus the very first Pan-African Architecture Biennale opened its doors on Monday in the Kenyan capital Nairobi.</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Tunisians tell the story of their summer of shortages</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:37fe91ed81452a0eb4daf7b9cebd8e5a0a0c4841c7a3e7b261016d08698f6529</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-07T18:50:16Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages--617d7548111f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260907-tunisians-tell-story-their-summer-shortages" /><summary type="html">Frequent water and power cuts, long queues for bottled water, poultry farms destroyed by the heat: these have been the scenes across Tunisia this summer as millions of people were affected by shortages in electricity and water. Our Observers say this is the result of years of underinvestment in infrastructure while the Tunisian president, for his part, blames "deliberate and planned acts of sabotage”.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Egyptian TV presenter Sarah Khalifa sentenced to death in drug case</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:0ae6eae6f844be900e188320cba69903cd3490b2c0c72300e2a49b9aae7ea883</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-06T13:52:42Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case--a5ffad89f8f8.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260906-egyptian-tv-presenter-sarah-khalifa-sentenced-to-death-in-drug-case" /><summary type="html">Sarah Khalifa, a well-known Egyptian TV presenter, has been sentenced to death for her involvement in a drug ring, state media reported over the weekend. Eleven others were also convicted in the case and handed a death sentence. Khalifa is best known for her program “Mission Impossible”.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry><entry><title>Uber withdraws from Nigeria and Uganda</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:5555dbad04c002b987b88a0c2c5d9e5ab8c1e9b93b9510cb82b94c2fab639e23</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T20:39:08Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria--7fddf4430022.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/uber-withdraws-from-nigeria" /><summary type="html">In tonight's programme: Uber is pulling out of Nigeria and Uganda, leaving thousands of drivers scrambling for new sources of income. But first: a UN report says foreign fighters and support networks are fuelling Sudan's war and worsening violence against civilians. Finally, we discuss the African Diaspora International Film Festival with co-director Diarah N'daw-Spech and Laurence Cole, co-producer of the Nigerian film "Shadow of Greed".</summary><category term="Eye on Africa" /></entry><entry><title>'Moroccans are desperate: Years of political crackdown and lack of economic opportunities'</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:ce6435a13a2cb73205d027ddfbbc71e9b540da596353ce8d11ce9e617a558c88</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T12:30:37Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities--0197ef1ebe1f.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/tv-shows/spotlight/20260904-moroccans-are-desperate-years-of-political-crackdown-and-lack-of-economic-opportunities" /><summary type="html">François Picard is pleased to welcome Aida Alami, New York Times World contributor. Drawing on her own field reporting at the Moroccan border, Alami challenges attempts to explain the mass crossing to the Spanish enclave of Ceuta primarily through a theory of deliberate state orchestration. While acknowledging both Morocco's history of leveraging migration in its relations with Spain and the possibility that security forces made tactical decisions on the day, Alami insists on distinguishing evidence from inference.</summary><category term="Spotlight" /></entry><entry><title>Mass burial reveals hidden toll of Algeria’s deadly ‘Black Wednesday’ wildfires</title><id>urn:rssync:entry:f4dee0e90303c601b4f0d4e24a9afd16cfdb6b83a1c43cbfe59e80abee8c1dd7</id><updated>2026-09-19T12:34:25Z</updated><published>2026-09-04T11:33:04Z</published><link rel="alternate" href="../../../../pages/www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires--e2ca45613885.html" type="text/html" /><link rel="via" href="https://www.france24.com/en/africa/20260904-mass-burial-algeria-black-wednesday-deadly-wildfires" /><summary type="html">Wildfires ripped across northeastern Algeria in late August. Photos and videos showing the mass burial of several dozen people have raised questions about the real death toll of the fires as Algerian authorities haven’t shared new numbers since August 26. A number of Moroccan social media accounts have also been sharing disinformation about the fires.</summary><category term="Africa" /></entry></feed>
